WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.250 --> 00:00:07.080
Malcolm Grant: Good morning everybody, and
welcome to this public meeting of NHS England.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:12.460
This is the first occasion on which the Board
has met under its new title, which took effect

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:15.420
from 1st April.

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:22.320
Also on 1st April, the new powers and responsibilities
of the organisation were conferred upon us.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:27.170
Up until now, we had been working as the NHS
Commissioning Board, and that remains the

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:31.410
formal legal title in the legislation.

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:38.870
The change of title (which we have agreed
with the Secretary of State) signifies a change

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:44.590
in name of the organisation, but the responsibilities
remain exactly as they were.

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:50.219
The title NHS England, by the way, has not
intended to assume responsibility for the

00:00:50.219 --> 00:00:51.219
whole of the NHS in England.

00:00:51.219 --> 00:00:57.930
There are other important organisations with
whom we work closely; CQC, the Care Quality

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:02.539
Commission, for example; NICE; and Monitor;
and Public Health England; and Health Education

00:01:02.539 --> 00:01:03.670
England.

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:10.000
There is an array of organisations with whom
we will work in the closest partnership, focused

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:18.350
very strongly on the needs of patients and
the transformation that the NHS needs to answer

00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:24.610
competently the criticisms of the Francis
Report, and also to secure safe and effective

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:29.890
healthcare across the nation.

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:36.780
The organisation NHS England has from the
beginning committed itself to being open and

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:40.040
transparent in everything that we do, hence
meeting in public.

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:45.970
It is not a public meeting in the sense that
it is an interactive opportunity, but we believe

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:51.759
that it is immensely important, given the
responsibilities that we now have, that the

00:01:51.759 --> 00:01:57.590
way in which the Board conducts itself and
the way in which it scrutinises future strategy

00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:00.640
and its implementation, needs to be conducted
in public.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:07.579
After all, it is on behalf of the public that
we undertake these important responsibilities.

00:02:07.579 --> 00:02:11.680
That does not mean that all of our business
is conducted in public.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:17.590
At the end of this meeting, we will resolve
to go into private session, because there

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:23.310
are some issues, for example, around procurement
and human resources, that need to be dealt

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:28.569
with on a confidential basis because there
is a commercial element of confidentiality.

00:02:28.569 --> 00:02:35.349
I would also add that we have been developing
the working of the Board of NHS England in

00:02:35.349 --> 00:02:41.650
such a way as to ensure that there is close
teamwork between the executive members and

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:48.040
the non-executive members, and that therefore,
from time to time, we have focused development

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:53.500
sessions to try to ensure that from the very
earliest stage of the development of new strategies

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:58.920
and policies, there is full engagement of
the non-executive directors side by side with

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:05.980
the executive directors, so that by the time
the strategy papers come to a meeting of the

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:11.010
full Board of NHS England, they have already
been through an iterative process between

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:17.970
executive and non-executive directors, and
we are not bringing cold new ideas to the

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:21.400
Board for discussion in public meetings.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:27.159
To my mind, that is a proper and sophisticated
way of organising the business of a Board

00:03:27.159 --> 00:03:29.879
such as this.

00:03:29.879 --> 00:03:35.049
This morningÕs business has an emphasis on
formality, to the extent that we are required

00:03:35.049 --> 00:03:40.180
as soon as possible after 1st April to ensure
that all of our financial and governance arrangements

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:41.180
are in order.

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:46.790
However, this morning, there are also some
immensely important items, particularly our

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:53.799
business plan, which sets out how it is that
we intend to take the lead on policy and strategy

00:03:53.799 --> 00:03:57.410
for the NHS in England over the coming years.

00:03:57.410 --> 00:04:04.540
The big change that occurs from 1st April
is that a significant proportion of the budget

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:12.330
for the NHS in England is allocated to us,
in part for reallocation Ð that is, to the

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:20.220
Clinical Commissioning Groups, which will
take around £65 billion of the total £95.6

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:28.330
billion of the Board of NHS England Ð but
in part also for around £25 billion for direct

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:34.000
commissioning by NHS England, both for specialist
services and for primary care.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:43.509
Therefore, NHS England now assumes an enormous
array of responsibility.

00:04:43.509 --> 00:04:51.550
On that, I would say that this is an exceptionally
exciting turning point for the overall governance

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:53.160
of the NHS in England.

00:04:53.160 --> 00:05:04.740
It is carrying with it, I think, an exceptional
range of responsibility and opportunity.

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:11.819
We are not an organisation that should over-promise
what we can achieve, but we will set out Ð

00:05:11.819 --> 00:05:16.990
you will hear some of it this morning Ð our
objectives and how we propose to go about

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:31.590
achieving them, and how we propose to track
our achievements and report on them publicly.

00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:54.220
I turn now to the formal business of the meeting.

00:05:54.220 --> 00:06:02.229
The first item is to ask the Board to approve
the minutes of the previous meeting.

00:06:02.229 --> 00:06:04.310
I take it that that is the case?

00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:05.960
Thank you very much.

00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:11.150
There is one matter arising from the minutes,
which relates to the proposal of delegated

00:06:11.150 --> 00:06:15.730
authority to the Chair and the Chief Executive
to sign a series of service-level agreements.

00:06:15.730 --> 00:06:22.380
I am advised that this has been handled in
a different way from that which we proposed,

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:27.860
which is that various service-level agreements
have been signed off within existing authorisations,

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:32.789
within existing financial allocations, and
within existing policies, so that it has not

00:06:32.789 --> 00:06:37.070
been necessary for us to activate the formal
delegation that the Board approved on the

00:06:37.070 --> 00:06:38.070
last occasion.

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:39.949
Let me check: Bill McCarthy, that is the case?

00:06:39.949 --> 00:06:41.930
Bill McCarthy: That is exactly right, Chairman.

00:06:41.930 --> 00:06:49.009
It is because there are about 400 contracts
that need to be signed, and the Chairman and

00:06:49.009 --> 00:06:56.690
Chief Executive having to sign all of those
would have been a pretty big job.

00:06:56.690 --> 00:07:01.850
Therefore, under existing authorities and
your delegations, we got agreement that they

00:07:01.850 --> 00:07:09.020
could be signed off by the regional and area
teams responsible, but importantly, within

00:07:09.020 --> 00:07:13.900
the financial parameters and within the policy
agreements that the Board was content with.

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:14.900
Malcolm Grant: Good.

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:15.900
Right, thank you very much.

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:18.460
Are there any other matters arising from the
minutes?

00:07:18.460 --> 00:07:19.460
No?

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:20.419
Okay, thanks very much.

